                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "I already had experience playing this \"game\" (if you can call it that) before a few years ago, when I was in a pretty dark phase of my life. I didn't understand why but the idea that you could just walk around, interact and become anything made me actually feel in control of my real life thoughts. I for once felt alive and fine. I cannot fully say what was that first feeling like as I already forgot the real emotions I felt but I remembered this game and some of its audios as important to me. So just recently I finally bought it on steam and while the feeling was different, this time my gameplay was being led by that nostalgia of comfort.\r\n\r\n A lot of what Alan Watts says in the audios felt like a hit or miss, as from my understanding he wasn't a scholar level of a philosopher but rather an entertainer so I don't have any real judgement. However, to a teen me, this was enough. I found his voice soothing, some words with understanding death/life (as I was passively thinking of dark thoughts) helped me ground myself. It felt like this game was just there in the right time of my life and I was grateful.\r\n\r\nTransforming into things our eyes cannot normally perceive was exciting: I love both astronomy and microbiology/chemistry so this was like a GOLD MINE of transformations, recognising the bacteria or any yeast which was more or less obvious. I like the silly movements, the dances. It's not a game with complex gaming or story, it just lets you interact with the world in any way you feel like doing so.\r\n\r\nNow to achievements- I managed to get all of them, it wasn't hard per-se but definitely took some time. Autoplay later on was useful as I was just working on anything else I needed to write (insane to think how I am still alive, now studying last year of my major... Life truly keeps going) and the longest achievements were related to \"self-thinking\" and erasing the thoughts multiple times. I genuinely cannot say if they accumulate how many thoughts or times you empty your mind. \r\n\r\nThe first time I entered the golden gate on my playthrough I never properly understood what was happening as I just... stupidly didn't play attention that they literally give you steps and everything. So THIS time I actually got to finish the tutorial lol and I like the small cutscene after you leave the golden gate :)\r\n\r\nOverall, NOT a game for everyone, as it's not something filled with action or a rich story. It's nice when you are bored or down, I liked reading the small thoughts and listening to some objects' sounds. It was amazing to me but that doesn't mean you will end up feeling the same way :D",
